.TH GIT-SPLICE 1 "" "git-splice" "Git Manual"
.SH NAME
git-splice \- keep folders in sync with their own upstream repositories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
git splice
git splice <command> [<args>]
git splice -h
git splice --version
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A splice is a folder of a repository that has its own upstream repository,
recorded in a committed .splice file in that folder.
Edit and commit spliced files alongside the rest of the monorepo, then
sync changes in either direction with the upstream repository.
The repositories keep separate histories; only file changes cross the splice.
.PP
By default, each splice syncs with the upstream branch named like the
monorepo's current branch. Commands read splices and their content from
the last commit; commit local edits before pushing them upstream.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Splicing in or out
These commands require splice paths, or --all where supported.
.TP
.B clone [--merge] <url> [<path>]
Splice an existing repository into the monorepo.
.TP
.B clone --from-submodule [--recursive] <path>
Turn a submodule into a splice.
.TP
.B init <path> <url>
Make an existing folder a splice of an empty upstream repository.
.TP
.B merge [--upstream <name>] (<path>... | --all)
Bring already-fetched upstream changes into the monorepo.
.TP
.B pull [--upstream <name>] (<path>... | --all)
Fetch upstream changes, then merge them.
.TP
.B push [--upstream <name>] [--force] (<path>... | --all)
Publish committed local changes upstream.
.SS Inspecting splices
These commands select every splice unless paths are given.
.TP
.B status [--upstream <name>] [<path>...]
Show each splice's sync state. Run fetch first for current upstream results.
.TP
.B diff [--upstream <name>] [--stat] [<path>...]
Show the file changes push would send.
.TP
.B log [--upstream <name>] [--graph] [<path>...]
Show the commits push and pull would move.
.TP
.B fetch [--upstream <name>] [<path>...]
Fetch the upstream repositories.
.SS Working with splice history
.TP
.B boundary <path>
Print the monorepo commit where the rebuild of a splice starts.
.TP
.B rebuild <path>
Print the upstream commit push would send.
.TP
.B key [--upstream <name>] <path> [<new-key>]
Print or rename the key of a splice's fetched refs.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -h
Print a brief usage summary and the command list.
.TP
.B --help
With no command, or when invoked as git splice --help, Git opens this manual.
Invoking git-splice --help directly prints the brief usage summary.
.TP
.B --version
Print the installed version.
.PP
Command options follow the command name. Use
.B git splice <command> -h
for the full options and help for that command.
Use this form also when no manual viewer is installed.
.PP
The shared command option
.B --upstream <name>
selects one of the upstreams named in .splice; without it, commands use
the splice's default upstream.
.SH EXAMPLES
Splice a library into the current repository:
.PP
.nf
    git splice clone https://example.com/library.git vendor/library
.fi
.PP
Inspect incoming changes and bring them into the monorepo:
.PP
.nf
    git splice fetch vendor/library
    git splice status vendor/library
    git splice pull vendor/library
.fi
.PP
After editing and committing files in vendor/library, inspect and publish them:
.PP
.nf
    git splice diff vendor/library
    git splice push vendor/library
.fi
.PP
Show detailed help for a command:
.PP
.nf
    git splice push -h
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1)
.PP
